 would be slope (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, which is allowed, but (3,2) is not a required point (since a+b ≤ 4, (3,2) has sum 5 > 4, not required). But we can still have a line passing through (2,1) and (3,2) even if (3,2) is not required; the line can pass through any points, but we need to cover required points. So we can choose any line that passes through (2,1) and some other point (maybe not required). That's okay. So we can have a sunny line L1 passing through (2,1) and (3,2). That line is slope 1, not parallel to axes or slope -1, so it's sunny. It covers (2,1). It also passes through (3,2) which is not required, but that's fine.\n\nNow we need to cover (2,2) and (3,1). We can have a sunny line L2 passing through (2,2) and (3,1). Slope = (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, which is slope -1, not allowed (sunny lines cannot be slope -1). So that's not allowed. Could we have L2 passing through (2,2) and some other point (maybe (3,3) which is not required)? (3,3) sum 6 > 4, not required. Slope = (3-2)/(3-2) = 1, allowed. So we can have L2 through (2,2) and (3,3). That covers (2,2). But we still need to cover (3,1). We have used both sunny lines already (L1 and L2). So (3,1) is uncovered. So this fails.\n\nAlternatively, choose non-sunny line vertical x=2. Then we cover (2,1),(2,2). Remaining points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1). Need to cover with two sunny lines. Column 1 has three points, column 3 has one point. Two sunny lines can cover at most two points in column 1, leaving one uncovered. So fails.\n\nNon-sunny line vertical x=3: covers (3,1). Remaining points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2). Columns: x=1 (3 points), x=2 (2 points). Two sunny lines can cover at most two points in column 1, leaving one uncovered. So fails.\n\nNon-sunny line horizontal y=1: covers (1,1),(2,1),(3,1). Remaining: (1,2),(1,3),(2,2). Columns: x=1 (2 points), x=2 (1 point). Two sunny lines can cover at most two points in column 1, which is okay (we have two points). They can also cover the point in column 2. So maybe possible. Let's try.\n\nWe need to cover (1,2),(1,3),(2,2) with two sunny lines. Column 1 has two points (1,2),(1,3). Column 2 has one point (2,2). Two sunny lines can each cover one point in column 1, and one of them can also cover the point in column 2 (if the line passes through a point in column 1 and the point in column 2). Let's try to find lines.\n\nLet L1 be a sunny line through (1,2) and (2,2). That's horizontal y=2, not allowed. So can't use that.\n\nL1 through (1,2) and (2,3) (point (2,3) not required). Slope = (3-2)/(2-1) = 1, allowed. So L1 covers (1,2). L2 must cover (1,3) and (2,2). Let's see if we can find a line through (1,3) and (2,2). Slope = (2-3)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. So can't use that.\n\nAlternatively, L1 through (1,2) and (2,4) (point (2,4) not required). Slope = (4-2)/(2-1) = 2, allowed. L2 through (1,3) and (2,2) is slope -1, not allowed. So we need to find a line that covers (2,2) and maybe also (1,3) but not slope -1. Let's try (2,2) and (1,4) (point (1,4) not required). Slope = (4-2)/(1-2) = 2/(-1) = -2, allowed. So L2 through (2,2) and (1,4) is sunny. It covers (2,2). So we have L1 covering (1,2), L2 covering (2,2). But (1,3) remains uncovered. We have used both sunny lines. So fails.\n\nAlternatively, we could try to cover (1,3) with L1 and (1,2) with L2, and (2,2) with one of them. Let's try L1 through (1,3) and (2,5) (point not required). Slope = (5-3)/(2-1) = 2, allowed. L2 through (1,2) and (2,2) is horizontal, not allowed. L2 through (1,2) and (2,3) slope = 1, allowed, but (2,3) not required. That covers (1,2). But (2,2) remains uncovered. So fails.\n\nThus with horizontal y=1 as non-sunny line, we cannot cover the remaining points with two sunny lines.\n\nNon-sunny line horizontal y=2: covers (1,2),(2,2). Remaining points: (1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1). Columns: x=1 (points (1,1),(1,3)), x=2 (point (2,1)), x=3 (point (3,1)). Two sunny lines can cover at most two points in column 1, which is okay (two points). They can also cover p