int (maybe (4,3) not required). Slope = (3-1)/(4-2) = 2/2 = 1, allowed. So L1 covers (2,1). L2 must cover (2,2) and (3,1). As before, line through (2,2) and (3,1) slope -1, not allowed. So fails.\n\nOption E: L1 passes through (3,1) and some other point (maybe (1,3) not required). Slope = (3-1)/(1-3) = 2/(-2) = -1, not allowed. So can't.\n\nOption F: L1 passes through (3,1) and (4,2) (point not required). Slope = (2-1)/(4-3) = 1, allowed. So L1 covers (3,1). L2 must cover (2,1) and (2,2). But line through (2,1) and (2,2) vertical, not allowed. So fails.\n\nThus this particular arrangement fails.\n\nMaybe we can choose a different non-sunny line instead of vertical x=1. Let's try using horizontal y=1 as non-sunny line. That covers (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1). Then we have remaining points: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,2),(2,3),(3,2). That's 6 points. We have 3 lines left: 2 sunny, 1 non-sunny. The non-sunny line could be vertical x=1 covering (1,2),(1,3),(1,4). Then remaining points: (2,2),(2,3),(3,2). Two sunny lines to cover these. Column 2 has (2,2),(2,3). Column 3 has (3,2). Two sunny lines can cover at most two points in column 2 (good) and one point in column 3 (good). Let's try to find lines.\n\nWe need to cover (2,2),(2,3),(3,2). As before, we need to assign points to lines. Column 2's two points must be assigned to different lines. So one line covers (2,2), the other covers (2,3). The line covering (3,2) can be either of them, but must be collinear with its assigned point(s). Let's try:\n\nLine L1: cover (2,2) and (3,2). That's slope = (2-2)/(3-2) = 0, horizontal, not allowed. So can't.\n\nLine L1: cover (2,2) and some other point (maybe (4,4) not required). Slope = (4-2)/(4-2) = 1, allowed. So L1 covers (2,2). L2 must cover (2,3) and (3,2). Slope = (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed. So fails.\n\nLine L1: cover (2,2) and (3,3) (point not required). Slope = (3-2)/(3-2) = 1, allowed. L2: cover (2,3) and (3,2). Slope = (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed. So fails.\n\nLine L1: cover